Setting Up Your Environment First, you need to prepare your computer to run Hyperledger Fabric. This involves installing some tools and software.
#1. Install Prerequisites 
#We Run this command in terminal using Linux system (Ubuntu -> 24.04.2) for installs tools like curl (for downloading files), 
git (for version control), docker and docker-compose (for running the network in containers), golang (for Go programming), make 
(for building software), and jq (for handling JSON data).
sudo apt install curl git docker.io docker-compose golang make jq -y

#Enable Docker service: Docker is like a virtual machine that runs the blockchain network. 
Enable and start the Docker service, and add your user to the Docker group so you can run Docker commands without needing sudo every time
sudo systemctl enable docker
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER

#2. Download Fabric Binaries and Samples. This includes the core programs (binaries), Docker images, and sample projects to get you started. 
Run this command from your home directory
cd ~ curl -sSL https://bit.ly/HyperledgerFabricInstaller | bash -s -- 2.5.4

#This installs:
Binaries: peer, orderer, fabric-ca-client, etc.
Docker images
Clones: fabric-samples/

#3. Launch the Test Network. Let’s fire up a simple test network to start with. 
This network will have two organizations (Org1 and Org2) and an orderer (the component that organizes transactions).
#Navigate to the test network directory:
cd ~/fabric-samples/test-network

#Start the network (Org1 + Org2 + Orderer): 
./network.sh up createChannel -c testchannel -ca

#4. Deploy Chaincode Deploying a Smart Contract (Chaincode) In Fabric, We’ll deploy a sample chaincode called asset-transfer-basic, 
written in Go, to manage assets on the blockchain.
#To deploy sample asset-transfer-basic chaincode:
./network.sh deployCC -ccn basic -ccp ../asset-transfer-basic/chaincode-go -ccl go -c testchannel

#5. Confirm Network and Chaincode Status. To check if the chaincode is installed on your peers (the nodes in Org1 and Org2)
#Check installed chaincode:
peer lifecycle chaincode queryinstalled

#Check committed chaincode:
peer lifecycle chaincode querycommitted --channelID testchannel
